\details{
Node.js-style event-driven base for Binance's public market-data WebSocket
streams. You register handlers with \verb{$on(event, handler)} — exactly like
\code{ws.on("message", ...)} in JavaScript — and the library calls them as messages
arrive on R's event loop (the \code{later} package, which, like Node, is built on
libuv). Subclasses such as \link{BinanceMarketStream} add typed stream methods.
\subsection{Why no \code{async} flag (unlike the REST classes)}{

A REST call has a single result, so it can return a value (sync) or a promise
(async). A socket is an endless push stream with no single result, so the only
sensible shape is a callback. There is therefore nothing to dualise: streams
are always event-driven. The one thing R needs that Node gives for free is a
way to keep the process alive and pump the loop — that is \verb{$run()}.
}

\subsection{Events}{
\itemize{
\item \code{"open"} — handler called (with the open event) once the socket connects.
\item \code{"message"} — handler called with the \strong{raw JSON string} of every message.
Parse it with \code{\link[jsonlite:fromJSON]{jsonlite::fromJSON()}} for structure, or write it straight to
disk for faithful archival.
\item \code{"close"} — handler called (with the close event) when the socket closes.
\item \code{"error"} — handler called (with the error event) on a socket error.
}
}

\subsection{Connection management (handled for you)}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Auto-reconnect} with full-jitter exponential backoff on an unexpected
close (so a reconnect storm can never trip Binance's connection rate limit).
\item \strong{Proactive reconnect at 23h}, before Binance's 24-hour forced disconnect.
\item \strong{Re-subscribe} every tracked stream after any reconnect.
\item \strong{Ping/pong} keepalive is answered automatically by the \code{websocket} package.
}
}

\subsection{Dependencies}{

Built on the \code{websocket} (the client) and \code{later} (R's libuv event loop)
packages, both hard \code{Imports} — no optional-dependency dance, they are always
present.
}
}
